<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Nevsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1221">1221</a>–<a href="/years/1263">1263</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander Nevsky was a Rus' prince who served as prince of Novgorod and from 1252 as Grand Prince of Vladimir. He defeated the Swedes on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> in 1240 and the knights of the Livonian Order on the ice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peipus">Lake Peipus</a> in 1242. He chose submission to the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> to protect his lands, and was later made a saint of the Russian Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky.jpg" alt="Alexander Nevsky" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Collective of Kremlin Armory artists · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlexanderNevskyTitul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexander was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a> on 13 May 1221, although an older tradition placed his birth on 30 May 1220. He was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir">Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</a>, who held Pereslavl-Zalessky and the throne of Novgorod during the Mongol conquest of 1237 to 1238 and inherited the Grand Principality of Vladimir in 1238 after the death of his elder brother Yury. There is no consensus on the identity of Alexander's mother, whom his vita names as Feodosiya.</p><p>Alexander is first mentioned directly in 1228, when his father left him and his elder brother to rule Novgorod. Fyodor died in the summer of 1233, aged 14, leaving Alexander the eldest surviving son. In 1239 he married Aleksandra, a daughter of the prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>, Bryacheslav Vasilkovich, and they had five children, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-of-pereslavl">Dmitry of Pereslavl</a>, Andrey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorodets">Gorodets</a> and Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1236 the Novgorod Republic appointed Alexander prince of Novgorod. His father chose him and the veche approved, since it needed his armies. He ruled there in 1236 to 1240, 1241 to 1256 and 1258 to 1259. Alexander continued his family's anti-Western views, which made him unpopular with the veche. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-sit-river">Battle of the Sit River</a> in 1238 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-ii-of-vladimir">Yuri II of Vladimir</a> was killed, Yaroslav received the khan's permission to become prince.</p><p>In 1240 the Swedes, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birger-jarl">Birger Jarl</a>, launched what is called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-swedish-crusade">Second Swedish Crusade</a>. According to Russian sources, their army landed at the confluence of the Izhora and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a>, and Alexander attacked it on 15 July 1240 and won the Battle of the Neva. The title Nevsky is attested from the 15th century. He also fought German crusaders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-ice">Battle on the Ice</a> in 1242. He agreed to pay tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, which allowed him to preserve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a>, and from 1252 to 1263 he was Grand Prince of Vladimir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1263 Alexander visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> on a diplomatic mission. He fell ill on the way back and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorodets">Gorodets</a> on 14 November 1263, and was buried in Vladimir on 23 November. Veneration began almost at once. His remains were found incorrupt in 1380 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macarius-metropolitan-of-moscow">Macarius, Metropolitan of Moscow</a>, canonised him in 1547. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> had the relics moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1724, and Catherine I founded the imperial Order of Saint Alexander Nevsky in 1725.</p><p>His image as defender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was used later, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergei-eisenstein">Sergei Eisenstein</a>'s 1938 film and by Soviet authorities during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, who introduced a Soviet Order of Alexander Nevsky in 1942. Critics argue that the size and importance of his victories were exaggerated for political purposes and that he helped ensure the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>'s dominance over Russia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander Nevsky” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the death of <b>Alexander Nevsky</b> in 1263 the town passed, according to tradition, to his youngest son Daniel, then a small child, and under him it became the seat of a separate line of princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Alexander Yaroslavich</b> defeated a Swedish force on the Neva in 1240, earning the name Nevsky, and in 1242 defeated the Livonian branch of the Teutonic Order on the ice of Lake Peipus, the Battle on the Ice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After that the grand princes fought chiefly as agents or rivals under Mongol overlordship, while <b>Alexander Nevsky</b>, before his accession, had defeated the Swedes on the Neva in 1240 and the Teutonic Knights on Lake Peipus in 1242.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Novgorod's forces under Prince <b>Alexander Nevsky</b> beat the Livonian branch of the Teutonic Order in the Battle on the Ice, halting the Order's eastward expansion into Russian lands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-5">5 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A Novgorodian army led by <b>Alexander Nevsky</b>, with Karelian allies, defeated a Swedish force that included Norwegian, Finnish and Tavastian troops on the Neva River near Ust-Izhora.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-15">15 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Tver, on the upper Volga, received its first prince around 1247, when Yaroslav Yaroslavich, a younger brother of <b>Alexander Nevsky</b>, made it his seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-tver">Grand Principality of Tver</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Gorodets, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.74,38.86&amp;year=1248" aria-label="Pereslavl-Zalessky on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alexander Nevsky"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5414 5251h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5414 5251h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.74,38.86&amp;year=1248">What was here in 1248? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
